static lstr L3232 = {0, 125, "Narra has no classes. Data goes in a struct and behavior in functions of the module.\012Fix: `class A {}` becomes `struct A {}`."};
static lstr L3233 = {0, 5, "E0924"};
static lstr L3234 = {0, 125, "Narra has no interfaces. Use a struct for a data shape and a trait for behavior.\012Fix: `interface A {}` becomes `struct A {}`."};
static lstr L3235 = {0, 5, "E0925"};
static lstr L3236 = {0, 153, "Narra has no `new`. A struct is built with a literal, other values with a function call.\012Fix: `new A()` becomes `A {}` when A is a struct without fields."};
static lstr L3237 = {0, 5, "E0926"};
static lstr L3238 = {0, 120, "Narra has no exceptions. A function that can fail returns `Result<T, E>`; the caller checks it with `is Err` or `match`."};
static lstr L3239 = {0, 5, "E0927"};
static lstr L3240 = {0, 124, "Narra has no `throw`. Return `Err(value)` from a function that returns a Result.\012Fix: `throw \"x\"` becomes `return Err(\"x\")`."};
static lstr L3241 = {0, 5, "E0928"};
static lstr L3242 = {0, 62, "Narra has no `this`. A function gets the value as a parameter."};
static lstr L3243 = {0, 5, "E0930"};
static lstr L3244 = {0, 583, "Narra has no function declarations inside a function. Declare the function at module level as a private function and pass the enclosing values as arguments.\012There is no automatic fix: moving it changes the meaning when its body reads a name that a local shadows, and breaks helpers that use the enclosing type parameters.\012For a helper that captures, bind an arrow function: `const helper = (x: int): int => x + offset`.\012Example: `function run() { function add(a: int): int { return a + 1 } }` becomes `function add(a: int): int { return a + 1 }` followed by `function run() { ... }`."};
static lstr L3245 = {0, 5, "E0991"};
static lstr L3246 = {0, 493, "A `@target` function exists only on its target, so a use must sit where only that target compiles it: inside an `if` on `Build.os` or `Build.arch` that the other targets drop, or inside a `@target` function with the same or a narrower target. An ordinary `if`, a `match` and an early `return` do not count.\012`narrac check` checks the host only; `narrac check --all-targets` checks every supported target.\012Example: `print(linuxName())` becomes `if (Build.os == Os.Linux) { print(linuxName()) }`."};
static lstr L3247 = {0, 5, "E0992"};
static lstr L3248 = {0, 440, "A constant type parameter is written `const N: int` in the type parameters of a function or a struct. Its kind is `int`; enums, type aliases and other integer kinds do not take one. A type argument is a type, an integer literal or a constant parameter in scope; it never computes.\012Example: `Vector<N + 1>` is rejected; pass the length as its own constant parameter, `function grow<const N: int, const M: int>(vector: Vector<N>): Vector<M>`."};
static lstr L3249 = {0, 5, "E0993"};
static lstr L3250 = {0, 362, "A constant parameter takes an integer literal or a constant parameter in scope, and a type parameter takes a type; the two do not mix. A local, a type or a top-level constant cannot stand for a constant parameter, and a constant cannot stand for a type.\012Example: `Vector<count>` becomes `Vector<3>`, and `value: N` becomes `value: int` with `N` read in the body."};
static lstr L3251 = {0, 5, "E0994"};
static lstr L3252 = {0, 527, "Indexing a FixedArray has no runtime check, so every index is proven at compile time. A constant index must be from 0 to N - 1. Any other index must be a plain name that a check puts in that range: an `if` or `while` condition such as `i >= 0 && i < 4`, the condition of `for (let i = 0; i < N; i++)`, or `for (const i of Ranges.upTo(N))`. An assignment to the name ends what the check proved. `FixedArrays.get(values, i)` returns an Option for any index.\012Example: `values[i]` becomes `if (i >= 0 && i < 4) { use(values[i]) }`."};
static lstr L3253 = {0, 5, "E0995"};
static lstr L3254 = {0, 366, "`FixedArray<T, N>` takes an element type and a length. The length is an integer literal or a constant type parameter, and never negative. One FixedArray value takes at most 1048576 bytes (1 MiB); use an Array for more. A literal builds a FixedArray when the expected type is one, and gives every element.\012Example: `const point: FixedArray<f64, 3> = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]`."};
static lstr L3255 = {0, 5, "E0996"};
static lstr L3256 = {0, 354, "A call of a std/ptr function or of a function with the unsafe effect may break memory safety, so it is allowed only inside an `unsafe { ... }` block. The function that holds the block gets the unsafe effect, unless it is marked `@trusted`.\012Example: `const value = Ptr.read(items)` becomes `let value = 0` followed by `unsafe { value = Ptr.read(items) }`."};
static lstr L3257 = {0, 5, "E0997"};
static lstr L3258 = {0, 328, "`@trusted` states that a function's `unsafe` blocks were checked by hand, and stops the unsafe effect there: callers need no `unsafe` block. A function without an `unsafe` block has nothing to trust, and a `@trusted` function does not declare `uses unsafe`.\012Example: remove `@trusted` from a function that has no `unsafe` block."};
static lstr L3259 = {0, 5, "E0998"};
static lstr L3260 = {0, 457, "A `Ptr<T>` is a raw pointer with no reference count. It may point at numbers, `bool`, `char`, another `Ptr` and structs whose fields are all such values, because a pointer to a string, array, map, box, enum or closure would bypass reference counting. The std/ptr functions take a `Ptr`; `Ptr.alloc`, `Ptr.null` and `Ptr.cast` take the element type from where the result goes.\012Example: `Ptr<string>` is rejected; `const items: Ptr<int> = Ptr.alloc(4)` works."};
static lstr L3261 = {0, 5, "E1000"};
static lstr L3262 = {0, 431, "C functions are declared in a block that names their library: `extern { function sqrt(value: f64): f64 } from \"m\"`. A member is a signature with no type parameters, no `uses` and no body; it has the unsafe effect. Only `@extern(\"name\")` may stand before a member. The libraries come from `from`, so `[link]` in narra.toml takes only `search`.\012Example: `[link] libraries = [\"m\"]` is removed; write `from \"m\"` after the extern block."};
static lstr L3263 = {0, 5, "E1001"};
static lstr L3264 = {0, 485, "A C function is linked by its C name, the member's name or the one `@extern` gives. That name must be a C identifier and must not be one the compiler writes itself: `main`, names starting with `narra_` or `lstr_`, string literal names such as `L1`, and the generated names of the program's functions and types, alone or with a helper's operation before or a suffix after (`release_E_Option_e088c655`).\012Example: `@extern(\"narra_alloc\")` is rejected; call the C function by its own name."};
static lstr L3265 = {0, 5, "E1002"};
static lstr L3266 = {0, 326, "A C function takes and returns values C can pass: the number types, `bool` and `Ptr<T>`, and may return nothing. Strings, arrays and other counted values have no C form; pass a `Ptr<u8>`, for a string `CStrings.pointer` of a CString.\012Example: `function strlen(text: string): u64` becomes `function strlen(text: Ptr<u8>): u64`."};
static lstr L3267 = {0, 5, "E1003"};
static lstr L3268 = {0, 302, "`typeof` of a constant needs the constant's type, and a constant without a written type has its type only after its value is checked. In a declaration of the same module (a signature, a field, an alias) that type is not known yet, so write it.\012Example: `const limit = 3` becomes `const limit: int = 3`."};
static lstr L3269 = {0, 5, "E1004"};
static lstr L3270 = {0, 205, "The compiler tracks a position as a line and a column below 1048576 each. A token past either limit is rejected, since it would share a record with another position. Split the file, or break the long line."};
static lstr L3271 = {0, 5, "E1010"};
static lstr L3272 = {0, 363, "A tuple element is read with a constant index, `pair[0]`, so the read is a field read and cannot fail. An index that is a `let`, a parameter or any other computed value is rejected, because the element type would depend on it. A top-level `const` works. To read every element, destructure the tuple.\012Example: `pair[count]` becomes `const #[first, second] = pair`."};
static lstr L3273 = {0, 5, "E1011"};
static lstr L3274 = {0, 252, "A tuple has a fixed number of elements, and a constant index must name one of them, counting from 0. Tuple elements are read-only, so `pair[0] = 2` is E0226.\012Example: for `#[int, string]`, the indexes are 0 and 1; `pair[2]` and `pair[-1]` are rejected."};
static lstr L3275 = {0, 5, "E1012"};
static lstr L3276 = {0, 379, "A distinct type has the representation of its base type, so a derived trait is the base type's: `distinct type UserId = int derives Show` prints as an int. The derive needs the base type to implement the trait.\012Example: `distinct type Handle = Ptr<int> derives Show` is rejected, since a pointer has no Show; write an `implement Show for Handle` block and list `implements Show`."};
static lstr L3277 = {0, 5, "E1013"};
static lstr L3278 = {0, 394, "A standard function is a value when its signature is fixed: `Int.toString`, `Str.length`, `Fs.readText`. A function whose types depend on the call (`Arrays.length`, `Maps.size`), one with a `mutable` parameter and one that takes a function argument have no single signature, so they are not values.\012Example: write the arrow function `(items) => Arrays.length(items)` instead of `Arrays.length`."};
static lstr L3279 = {0, 5, "E1014"};
static lstr L3280 = {0, 352, "`==`, `!=` and map keys on a distinct type compare and hash the base value, so an `implement Equatable` or `implement Hashable` block for it would never run and is rejected. Write the comparison as a function of the declaring module, or wrap the value in a struct.\012Example: `implement Equatable for UserId` is rejected for `distinct type UserId = int`."};
static lstr L3281 = {0, 5, "E1020"};
static lstr L3282 = {0, 451, "A struct or enum can require traits of its type parameters, as in `struct Labeled<T: Show>`. Every use of the type with arguments must meet them: a written type, a struct literal and an enum constructor. A type parameter of the enclosing function or type meets a constraint only when it declares the same trait; constraints are not implied.\012Example: `function first<T>(pair: Labeled<T>)` is rejected; write `function first<T: Show>(pair: Labeled<T>)`."};
static lstr L3283 = {0, 5, "E1021"};
static lstr L3284 = {0, 427, "An implement block for a generic struct or enum names the type with the type parameters of its declaration, by the same names and in the same order. The block adds no constraints of its own: the members see the constraints the declaration gives, and the block holds for every instance.\012Example: for `struct Pair<T>`, `implement Show for Pair<int>` and `implement Show for Pair` are rejected; write `implement Show for Pair<T>`."};
static lstr L3285 = {0, 5, "E1022"};
static lstr L3286 = {0, 218, "`Dynamic<Trait>` holds a value of any type that implements one trait, so its one type argument is a trait name. `Dynamic<int>`, `Dynamic` alone and `Dynamic<Show, Ordered>` are rejected.\012Example: write `Dynamic<Show>`."};
static lstr L3287 = {0, 5, "E1023"};
static lstr L3288 = {0, 488, "A call through a `Dynamic<Trait>` passes the Dynamic where the trait writes `Self`, and the table of the stored type does the rest. That works only when every function of the trait takes `Self` as its first parameter, once and not inside another type, and mentions `Self` nowhere else, the result included. `Equatable` and `Ordered` take two values, and `Shape.fromValue` returns `Self`.\012Example: `trait Drawable { function draw(value: Self): string }` can be used as `Dynamic<Drawable>`."};
static lstr L3289 = {0, 5, "E1024"};
static lstr L3290 = {0, 236, "`Dynamic.of(value)` takes the trait from the expected type: an annotation, a parameter type, a field type or a result type. It takes one value and is the only function of `Dynamic`.\012Example: `const item: Dynamic<Show> = Dynamic.of(42)`."};
static lstr L3291 = {0, 5, "E1025"};
static lstr L3292 = {0, 265, "Every copy of a Dynamic shares one stored value, so a trait function that takes `mutable value: Self` cannot be called through a Dynamic: the change would reach the other copies.\012Example: call the function on the concrete value before wrapping it with `Dynamic.of`."};
static lstr L3293 = {0, 5, "E1050"};
static lstr L3294 = {0, 485, "The compiler runs an @const function while it compiles and writes the result into the C, so its parameters and result hold only values the compiler can build and write: numbers, bool, char, string, bytes, Array, Map, FixedArray, Option, Result, tuples, and structs, enums and distinct types made of these. A function value, Cell, Ptr, Shared, a generator, a trait-constrained type parameter or an effect parameter has no compile-time value.\012Fix: remove @const, or change the signature."};
static lstr L3295 = {0, 5, "E1051"};
static lstr L3296 = {0, 216, "An @const function runs inside the compiler, where nothing outside it can observe what it does. It may allocate, and nothing else: no io and no unsafe code.\012Fix: move the effect out of the function, or remove @const."};
static lstr L3297 = {0, 5, "E1052"};
static lstr L3298 = {0, 513, "An @const function calls other @const functions, constructors and the std functions of std/string, std/array, std/map, std/int, std/float, std/range, std/char, std/option, std/result, std/regex, std/math, std/bytes, std/fixedarray, std/f32 and the sized number modules that take no function. It cannot use an arrow function, call through a function value or run a user implement block (through ==, a map key or a direct call).\012Fix: mark the called function @const, compute the value another way, or remove @const."};
static lstr L3299 = {0, 5, "E1053"};
static lstr L3300 = {0, 329, "The compiler computes a constant that calls an @const function while it compiles. A panic, a failed assert, an index out of range or a std function that would panic stops the computation, and the error shows the chain of calls from the constant to where it stopped.\012Fix: change the arguments or the function so it does not panic."};
static lstr L3301 = {0, 5, "E1054"};
static lstr L3302 = {0, 376, "A constant may take 10 million evaluation steps (one per expression, statement and loop turn, plus one per 8 bytes or elements a std call goes through), so a mistake such as an endless loop cannot hang the compiler.\012Fix: compute a table this large at run time in a function, or generate a source file; for Regexes.checked, compile the pattern at run time with Regexes.compile."};
static lstr L3303 = {0, 5, "E1055"};
static lstr L3304 = {0, 493, "The values a constant builds while it is computed may take 64 MiB, counted as 8 bytes per number, element, field and entry, string lengths rounded up to 8, and 16 per array and map; `copy(...)` and storing a value read from a place count its outer level again. A std call whose result would pass the limit is stopped before it runs.\012Fix: compute a table this large at run time in a function, or generate a source file; for Regexes.checked, compile the pattern at run time with Regexes.compile."};
static lstr L3305 = {0, 5, "E1056"};
static lstr L3306 = {0, 344, "A constant may make 1000 nested calls while it is computed, nest calls, blocks, loops and expressions 5000 deep, and build values nested 1000 levels deep, which keeps the compiler stack safe. A function that calls itself as its last step (`return f(...)`) runs as a loop and does not count.\012Fix: write the recursion as a loop or as a tail call."};
static lstr L3307 = {0, 5, "E1057"};
static lstr L3308 = {0, 202, "An @const function may read top-level constants. A constant whose computation reads the constant itself has no value to start from.\012Fix: break the cycle, for example by passing the value as an argument."};
static lstr L3309 = {0, 5, "E1100"};
static lstr L3310 = {0, 326, "`await` waits for a task, so it can stand only where the body is itself a task: an async function, an async arrow or an async block. A `function*` body and an arrow that is not async are bodies of their own, also inside an async body.\012Fix: make the function or arrow async, or pass the handle on and await it in an async body."};
static lstr L3311 = {0, 5, "E1102"};
static lstr L3312 = {0, 146, "`await` takes the handle of a task, an Async<T>, and gives its value.\012Fix: await the call that returns the handle, or use the value without await."};
static lstr L3313 = {0, 5, "E1103"};
static lstr L3314 = {0, 322, "A call of an async function, an async arrow or an `async { ... }` block starts a task and gives its handle. A handle that nobody uses hides a task that keeps running; `const _ =` does not let it go.\012Fix: await the handle, keep it in a name, pass it on, or call Async.detach(handle) to let the task run with nobody waiting."};
static lstr L3315 = {0, 5, "E1104"};
static lstr L3316 = {0, 177, "A task outlives the call that starts it, so it cannot keep a pointer into the caller's variable.\012Fix: take the value as a plain parameter and return the new value from the task."};
static lstr L3317 = {0, 5, "E1105"};
static lstr L3318 = {0, 313, "A `for (mutable ...)` loop holds a pointer to the element it changes, which cannot be kept in a task frame across an await. `for await (mutable ...)` is rejected too: the values of a stream are not elements of a place.\012Fix: change the elements through an index; read a stream with `for await (const x of stream)`."};
static lstr L3319 = {0, 5, "E1106"};
static lstr L3320 = {0, 162, "A handle is an identity type: copies share one task, so == and a map key would compare tasks, not values.\012Fix: await the handles and compare or key by the values."};
static lstr L3321 = {0, 5, "E1107"};
static lstr L3322 = {0, 103, "retired: `async function*` declares an async generator now (D110), so this error is no longer reported."};
static lstr L3323 = {0, 5, "E1108"};
static lstr L3324 = {0, 135, "`async` starts an async function, an async arrow or an async block.\012Fix: write `async function`, `async (x) => ...` or `async { ... }`."};
static lstr L3325 = {0, 5, "E1109"};
static lstr L3326 = {0, 139, "Async<T> takes the type of the task's value as its one type argument.\012Fix: write Async<T>, with Async<void> for a task that gives no value."};
static lstr L3327 = {0, 5, "E1110"};
static lstr L3328 = {0, 284, "An async arrow or block copies what it captures when its task starts, so a `let`, a `mutable` parameter or a captured value it changes would no longer be shared with the code around it.\012Fix: keep state that changes in a Cell from std/cell, or pass the value in and return the new one."};
static lstr L3329 = {0, 5, "E1111"};
static lstr L3330 = {0, 174, "An async function keeps its parameters in its task, which outlives the call, so a function parameter must be one that may be stored.\012Fix: declare the parameter as `escaping`."};
static lstr L3331 = {0, 5, "E1112"};
static lstr L3332 = {0, 292, "An `async function*` produces a stream of values that may wait between them, read with `Async.next` or `for await`. Its result names that stream, with the effects of the body on it.\012Fix: write the result as `AsyncGenerator<T>`, as in `async function* lines(): AsyncGenerator<string> uses io`."};
static lstr L3333 = {0, 5, "E1113"};
static lstr L3334 = {0, 241, "An AsyncGenerator<T> body may wait between its values, so only an async generator can return one from its own body. A plain or async function may still return a stream it got elsewhere.\012Fix: write `async function*`, or return a Generator<T>."};
static lstr L3335 = {0, 5, "E1114"};
static lstr L3336 = {0, 120, "AsyncGenerator<T> takes the type of the values the stream yields as its one type argument.\012Fix: write AsyncGenerator<T>."};
static lstr L3337 = {0, 5, "E1115"};
static lstr L3338 = {0, 202, "An async generator yields values to its readers and gets nothing back: there is no async coroutine.\012Fix: write `yield value` as a statement, and pass input to the stream through its arguments or a Cell."};
static lstr L3339 = {0, 5, "E1116"};
static lstr L3340 = {0, 231, "`yield*` in an async generator passes on every value of another AsyncGenerator<T> with the same T, one per step of the outer stream.\012Fix: delegate to an AsyncGenerator<T>; for a Generator<T>, write `for (const x of g) { yield x }`."};
static lstr L3341 = {0, 5, "E1122"};
static lstr L3342 = {0, 366, "`for await` waits for each value of a stream, so it can stand only where the body is itself a task: an async function, an async arrow, an async block or an async generator. A `function*` body and an arrow that is not async are bodies of their own, also inside an async body.\012Fix: make the function or arrow async, or read the stream with Async.next in an async body."};
static lstr L3343 = {0, 5, "E1123"};
static lstr L3344 = {0, 265, "`for await` reads an AsyncGenerator<T>, the stream an `async function*` gives, one step at a time.\012Fix: for a Generator<T> or an array, write `for (const x of items)`, which works in an async body; for an Array<Async<T>>, loop over the handles and `await` each one."};
static lstr L3345 = {0, 5, "E1124"};
static lstr L3346 = {0, 197, "The values of an AsyncGenerator<T> may wait, so a plain `for ... of` cannot read them.\012Fix: write `for await (const x of stream)` in an async body, or read one step with `await Async.next(stream)`."};
static lstr L3347 = {0, 5, "E1150"};
static lstr L3348 = {0, 347, "An Async<void> handle binds the type parameter of its Async<T> to void. A generic function whose signature also uses that parameter as a value, such as Async.peek (Option<T>), Async.all (Array<T>) or Async.race (Option<T>), would need a void value, which does not exist.\012Fix: await the handles one by one, or give the task a value, such as a bool."};
static lstr L3349 = {0, 5, "E1190"};
static lstr L3350 = {0, 522, "A value that crosses to another thread (a capture or the result of a thread function, the items of Parallel.map) must be Sendable. Sendable is automatic: a type is Sendable when nothing in it, at any depth, is a function, a Dynamic, a Ptr, a Cell, an Async, a generator or coroutine, a Scope, a Thread or a socket handle. These hold state that one thread changes in place without a lock.\012Fix: share changing state through Shared from std/shared, send plain data, and declare a type parameter that crosses as <T: Sendable>."};
static lstr L3351 = {0, 5, "E1191"};
static lstr L3352 = {0, 500, "A Scope joins its threads when its Thread.scope returns, so it must not outlive that call. It can be passed to functions, but not stored in a field, element, tuple or constructor, returned, captured by an escaping closure such as a thread function, or kept in the frame of a generator, coroutine, stream or task (a parameter or a capture), which can run after Thread.scope returns.\012Fix: pass the Scope as a parameter to the function that starts threads, or open a new Thread.scope where it is needed."};
static lstr L3353 = {0, 5, "E1192"};
static lstr L3354 = {0, 230, "A thread runs one plain function. An async function would give an Async handle, which belongs to the event loop of the thread that made it.\012Fix: write a plain function; it may start tasks, which its thread finishes before it ends."};
static lstr L3355 = {0, 5, "E1193"};
static lstr L3356 = {0, 329, "The checker must see what a thread function captures, and a function value does not show its captures in its type. So the function passed to Thread.start or Parallel.map is an arrow function written at the call or the name of a top-level function.\012Fix: write the arrow at the call, as in `Thread.start(scope, () => work(input))`."};
static lstr L3357 = {0, 5, "E1194"};
static lstr L3358 = {0, 231, "Sendable is only automatic: the compiler implements it for every type whose parts can cross a thread, so a type cannot list, derive or implement it.\012Fix: remove the `implement Sendable`, `implements Sendable` or `derives Sendable`."};
static lstr L3359 = {0, 5, "E1195"};
static lstr L3360 = {0, 263, "A Thread, a Scope, a Sender, a Receiver, a Shared or an Atomic is a handle shared by identity, so `==`, `!=` and map keys on it are errors, as for a Cell and an Async.\012Fix: compare the values the handles give, for example the results of Thread.join or Shared.get."};
static lstr L3361 = {0, 5, "E1200"};
static lstr L3362 = {0, 316, "A template right after a name is a tagged template: `tag`a${x}b`` calls `tag([\"a\", \"b\"], [x])`. Only a name or a dotted name can be the tag, so the function called is named where it is used.\012Fix: put the tag in a constant first (`const tag = make()`, then `tag`...``), or write a space-free name before the template."};
static lstr L3363 = {0, 5, "E1201"};
static lstr L3364 = {0, 317, "A tag is a function of two parameters: the parts, `Array<string>` (escapes decoded) or `Template.Raw` from std/template (the source text), and the values, `Array<T>`. Neither parameter can be `mutable`.\012Fix: give the tag that signature, for example `function tag(parts: Array<string>, values: Array<string>): string`."};
static lstr L3365 = {0, 5, "E1202"};
static lstr L3366 = {0, 231, "Every `${}` value of a tagged template has the tag's value type T, the element type of its second parameter. A generic tag takes T from the first value.\012Fix: convert the value inside the `${}`, for example `${Int.toString(count)}`."};
static lstr L3367 = {0, 5, "E1203"};
static lstr L3368 = {0, 249, "A line break ends the statement, so a template at the start of a line is not tagged by the name at the end of the line before, as it would be in TypeScript.\012Fix: write the tag and the template on one line, `const x = tag`a``, or remove the template."};
static lstr L3369 = {0, 5, "E1240"};
static lstr L3370 = {0, 488, "In a type, `uses` takes one effect, or a comma list in parentheses: `() => int uses io`, `Generator<int> uses (io, alloc)`. A `,` after the one effect ends the type, so the type can stand in a list of types or parameters. A function's own list, after its result type, is a bare comma list.\012Fix: put the type's effects in parentheses, `Generator<string> uses (io, alloc)`. A function that makes a generator writes its own list after it: `(): Generator<string> uses (io, alloc) uses alloc`."};
static lstr L3371 = {0, 5, "E1241"};
static lstr L3372 = {0, 377, "`io`, `alloc`, `unsafe` and effect variables name effects, which only a `uses` list holds; they are not types. In a list of types such as `Map<Generator<int> uses io, alloc>`, the `,` ends the generator's one effect, so `alloc` stands where a type belongs.\012Fix: write the effects in parentheses, `Map<Generator<int> uses (io, alloc), int>`, or move the name into a `uses` list."};
static lstr L3373 = {0, 5, "E1242"};
static lstr L3374 = {0, 189, "A `uses` list names each effect once, in a type and in a function's own list.\012Fix: delete the repeated name: `uses (io, io)` in a type and `uses io, io` on a function both become `uses io`."};
static lstr L3375 = {0, 5, "E1360"};
static lstr L3376 = {0, 428, "Regexes.checked compiles its pattern while the compiler runs, so a bad pattern is a compile error (E1053) instead of a panic. That works only where the compiler computes the value: the value of a top-level const. Anywhere else, including a function body or a use as a function value, the call would run in the program and could panic.\012Fix: move the call into a top-level const, or call Regexes.compile and handle its RegexError."};
static lstr L3377 = {0, 5, "E1410"};
static lstr L3378 = {0, 282, "Shared.update and Shared.read hold the lock while their callback runs. An async callback would give an Async whose task runs later, after the lock is released, or suspend while other threads wait for the lock.\012Fix: pass a plain function, and await the work before or after the call."};
static lstr L3379 = {0, 5, "E1411"};
static lstr L3380 = {0, 288, "An Atomic<T> uses the processor's atomic operations, which work on bool and the integer types up to 64 bits (int, i8 to i32, u8 to u64). A type parameter is not allowed either, since no bound says \"an integer type\".\012Fix: use an integer type, or keep the value in a Shared from std/shared."};
static lstr L3381 = {0, 5, "E1412"};
static lstr L3382 = {0, 141, "Atomic.add adds to an integer. An Atomic<bool> has no sum.\012Fix: use Atomic.store, Atomic.exchange or Atomic.compareExchange to change a flag."};
static lstr L3383 = {0, 5, "E1500"};
static lstr L3384 = {0, 335, "A statement after `return`, `break`, `continue`, a call to `panic` or `Process.exit`, an `if` and `else` that both leave, a `match` whose arms all leave, or a `while (true)` loop without a `break` never runs.\012Fix: delete the statements from the first dead one to the end of the block, or move the code before the statement that leaves."};
static lstr L3385 = {0, 5, "E1510"};
static lstr L3386 = {0, 243, "A `let` tells the reader that the name gets a new value later. A name that is never assigned, never changed with `+=` or `++`, and never has a field, an element or a `mutable` argument written is a `const`.\012Fix: write `const` instead of `let`."};
